  The current metrics work covers invariant observation — constraint graph walks that emit naturalist.observation and naturalist.invariant.violation counters automatically.

  The next step is a fluent conditional metric hook — domain code that fires a metric when a domain condition is met, outside the invariant observation framework. Example: a soil moisture reading crosses a threshold, a sensor value is out of range, a nutrient level exceeds safe limits. The pattern should be something like:

  Metric.counter("naturalist.soil.moisture.out.of.range")
      .tag("zone", zone.name().value())
      .tag("sensor", sensorId.value())
      .when(reading.percentVolumetric() > 45.0)
      .incrementCounter();

  The .when(boolean) (or whatever the right fluent verb is) should short-circuit — if the condition is false, incrementCounter() is a no-op. This keeps the metric declaration co-located with the domain logic without wrapping it in an if-block.

  Design constraints: Metric is lightweight and mutable. The consumer never sees MetricRegistry or Counter. The observability framework must never throw.




  Refactor remaining domains: name–display separation per ADR-005 Amendment 3

  The chemistry domain has already been refactored. The pattern established:

  - name() is the machine key — lower-kebab-case slug, validated by CatalogName (^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$ + per-subclass maxLength())
  - Human-readable values (titles, common names) are separate String fields on the entity record
  - CompoundCommonName (an EntityName subclass) was deleted — Compound.commonName became @UniqueValue String because common names are display labels,
  not cross-domain reference keys

